---
/**
 * Who writes this.
 *
 * The audit called this the single biggest humanising change available, and
 * the reasoning holds: a site whose whole argument is "a person went and
 * checked" needs the person to be findable. No stock photo, no invented
 * credentials, no claim to be a local. What's here is checkable, which is the
 * same standard the rest of the site is held to.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    mainEntity: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: "Matt Werth",
      jobTitle: "Software engineer",
      worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Werth Design", url: "https://werth.design" },
      description:
        "Writes and checks the Deep Creek Field Guide. Forty years going to Garrett County, first as a 4-H camper, later as a volunteer.",
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Who writes this — Deep Creek Field Guide"
  description="Matt Werth writes and checks this site. Forty years going out to Garrett County, a taste for tables and dates, and a habit of publishing the mistakes."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">About</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[14ch] text-slate">Who writes this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      Matt Werth. One person, not a team, and not a content operation.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I've been going to Garrett County for forty years. I started as a camper
      at what everyone still calls Pleasant Valley, went back as a volunteer,
      and I'm now the one driving out with kids in the back and a boot full of
      their stuff. That's the honest description of my standing here. I'm not a
      resident. I'm also not passing through.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I write software for a living and run <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth
      Design</a>, a studio in Wilmington, Delaware. Werth Design builds and pays
      for this site. Nothing on it is sponsored today. If that ever changes, the
      page it affects will say so.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I built it</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I drove to a park with a car full of children and found the thing I'd read
      about was wrong. That's the whole origin story. It isn't dramatic and it
      happens to everyone.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      What annoyed me wasn't the wrong fact. It was that there was no way to
      tell how old the answer was, or who'd said it. A page written in 2019 and
      a page written last week look identical.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      So the rule here is that every fact carries a date and a source, and where
      I haven't checked something it says so instead of guessing. It makes the
      site look less complete than its competitors. That's the point.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I ring the people who set the rule. Maryland DNR for a state park, the
      town for a town park, the organiser for an event. I write down the date I
      asked and who answered. If I only read it from an official page rather
      than asking a person, the page says that too, because they aren't the same
      claim.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I'm an engineer, so this ends up being mostly tables. I don't trust my own
      memory of a park I visited two summers ago and I don't expect you to.
    </p>

    <h2>I get things wrong in public</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Three so far, all still on the site with the date and what I used to say.
      The Swallow Falls reservation rule. The 4-H centre's swimming, which I had
      as open to anyone when it's for camp programmes. And three parks I'd
      marked "believed free", which is a guess wearing a fact's clothes.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <a href="/how-we-verify/">The method page has all three</a>. Leaving them
      up is not modesty. A guide that promises checking and quietly rewrites its
      errors is worth less than one that never promised.
    </p>

    <h2>Tell me when I'm wrong</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There's a report box at the bottom of every park page, and it takes about
      ten seconds. People who live there know these places better than I do, and
      a note from someone who stood in the car park yesterday beats another
      phone call from me.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Most of what's been fixed recently came in that way.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/how-we-verify/">How I check things, and what I've got wrong</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
